# The grammar each language's catalogs give Skwir's nouns (ADR-139, 5;
# docs/invariants/words.md, "Nouns"), held to by the catalogs' test
# (crates/core/src/words/tests.rs). The program reads nothing of it: a
# noun's attributes are its catalog's, said where a sentence names it.
#
# One language a line, by its catalogs' tag:
# - `genders:` the values of a noun's `.gender`, each a variant a sentence
#   chooses by `GENDER($place)` beside its default, `*[other]`, which is
#   what a name, a path or text as written takes;
# - `forms:` the forms a sentence takes a noun in, `FORM($place, case:
#   "…")`, each an attribute every noun of the language is given.
#
# Named as CLDR's grammatical features name them where it names them
# (masculine, feminine, neuter, common; nominative, accusative, dative,
# genitive, locative, instrumental…; definite: the noun with its article).
# A form ending in `+` holds a preposition the language fuses with the
# noun's article (French « du », de and le), which a name has not: a
# sentence takes it only in a GENDER variant other than `*[other]`, and
# says in `*[other]` what a name takes. A form a name has (a case, the
# noun with its article) is the name itself.
#
# A language not named here gives its nouns nothing, and its sentences
# choose by no gender.

fr  genders: masculine feminine  forms: definite de+
